--TEST--
`Core\Command::run` calls the handler the command line's first word names, with the arguments it matched — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 6's one deliberate divergence from `rule:routing/routes-are-compiled-not-registered`: this table *dispatches*. The
// handler is a `public static` method, and the compiled table carries it as a
// `Class::method` string, so nothing here names it a second time — the word
// `greet` below selects it and the words past that fill its parameters.
//
// `string $name` arrives as the text that was written and the `#[Core\Option]
// bool $loud` is a flag, given by being written rather than by carrying a
// value. The handler's own `uint` is what the process should exit with, and it
// is what `run` answers.
class Greeter {
    #[Core\Command(name: "greet", about: "Greet somebody by name")]
    public static function greet(string $name, #[Core\Option] bool $loud = false): uint {
        string $greeting = "Hello, " . $name . "!";
        if ($loud) {
            $greeting = Core\Str::upper($greeting);
        }
        echo "greet: ", $greeting, "\n";
        return 3;
    }
}

// The status is taken first, because the handler's own output is written while
// `run` is still being evaluated: `echo "status: ", Core\Command::run()` would
// print the label before the line the handler echoes.
uint $status = Core\Command::run();
echo "status: ", $status, "\n";
--ARGS--
greet
ada lovelace
--loud
--EXPECT--
greet: HELLO, ADA LOVELACE!
status: 3
